# pins for phase-1 transcripts consumed by circuits/setup.sh (PTAU_SOURCE=phase1|file:)
#
#   <sha256>  <filename>   [provenance note recorded by setup.sh on first fetch]
#
# Keyed by filename, so a transcript supplied as file:<path> pins under its own
# name and can never shadow (or be shadowed by) another file's entry at the same
# power. A pin means: this exact byte string passed `snarkjs powersoftau verify`
# — the full contribution chain — on a machine that ran setup.sh against it.
#
# What a pin does NOT mean: that anyone compared the contribution list it
# printed against the ceremony's published attestations. Until that audit is
# done by a second party, every pin here is trust-the-machine, and
# deployments/DEVELOPMENT_FIXTURE.md still says exactly what it says.
#
# Context for why pins are recorded rather than downloaded from a list: the
# buckets that served powersOfTau28_hez_final_* (the Hermez S3 bucket and the
# zkevm Google Cloud mirror both named by the snarkjs README) answer anonymous
# GETs with AccessDenied as of 2026-09-14 at the latest — an open upstream
# issue (iden3/snarkjs #636) reports every listed file, no resolution yet. A
# repo that simply re-pointed at a random mirror would be trading one thing to
# trust for a worse one; the two checks setup.sh performs (chain verification,
# pinned bytes) are what make any supplier of a transcript auditable at all.
#
# (no pins as of this writing — one demo entry recorded while exercising the
# file: mode was removed; the mechanism it tested is unchanged)
